def LAD(*a): return list(a)
def RUB(a,b,c,d): return [(a,'Missing or unclear','Meets the stated check',b),(c,'Guessing','Evidence kept',d),('Records','Gaps','Complete','Complete and reviewed by a peer'),('Safety and ethics','Overlooked','Followed','Followed and taught to others')]
COURSES = [
dict(slug='graphic-design', title='Graphic Design for Print and Screen', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Design clear layouts, logos and social posts with type, colour and grid, and deliver files that print and display well.',
 outs=[('Define','visual hierarchy and the parts of a design brief.'),('Apply','type, colour, grid and spacing to real layouts.'),('Analyse','a design against its brief and its audience.'),('Evaluate','readability, contrast and accessibility with measures.'),('Create','a brand kit with a logo, colours, type and three layouts.')],
 task='Create a small brand kit with a logo, colour palette, type pairing and three layouts, and deliver export-ready print and screen files.', cond='About 10 weeks part-time. A computer, free design software, a practice client or shop, a mentor.',
 standard='Each design answers a written brief, text meets a contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1, body text is at least 9 points in print and 16 pixels on screen, and files export at the correct size, resolution and colour mode.', who='Students, freelancers, shop owners, office staff who make posters and posts.', req='Basic computer skills.',
 mods=[
 M('Read a brief and know the audience','Analyse',4,['Write the goal, audience, message, size and deadline of the job in five lines.','Ask the client three questions: who sees it, where, and what should they do next.','Collect three examples of similar work and note what works in each.','Agree what is in scope and how many rounds of changes are included.'],'A sweet shop wants a festival poster. Brief: A3 size, seen from 3 metres, shoppers aged 25 to 55, action is to order by phone before the 10th.','Turn a vague request into a one-page brief and get the client to confirm it.','Brief states goal, audience, size, deadline and one action, and the client has approved it.','Why write a brief before opening software?','So both sides agree what success is and rework is avoided.'),
 M('Visual hierarchy and layout','Apply',5,['Choose one main message and make it the largest item, about twice the size of the next level.','Use three levels only: headline, support text and detail.','Group related items close together and leave more space between groups.','Test by squinting: the main message must still be seen first.'],'On a 210 by 297 mm flyer, the headline is 48 points, the offer line 24 points and the address 11 points; the order matches importance.','Design one flyer and run the squint test with three people.','Three of three viewers name the main message first within five seconds.','What is the squint test?','Blurring your eyes to see which item stands out first.'),
 M('Typography','Apply',5,['Pick two typefaces at most: one for headings and one for body text.','Set body text at 9 to 11 points in print and at least 16 pixels on screen.','Set line spacing at about 1.4 times the text size and keep lines near 45 to 75 characters.','Align text consistently and avoid all capitals for long text.'],'Body text at 10 points with 14 points line spacing in a column 70 characters wide reads easily; the same text in a 120 character line tires the eye.','Set a one-page article with a heading, subheading and three paragraphs.','Type uses two faces, correct sizes and line length within 45 to 75 characters.','Why limit the number of typefaces?','Many faces look messy and weaken hierarchy.'),
 M('Colour and contrast','Evaluate',5,['Build a palette of one main colour, one support colour and one neutral, plus black or white.','Check text against background using a contrast ratio tool; body text needs at least 4.5 to 1.','Do not rely on colour alone to show meaning; add a label or shape.','Remember that screens use red, green and blue while print uses cyan, magenta, yellow and black.'],'Light grey text on white measures 2.3 to 1 and fails; changing to dark grey gives 7 to 1 and passes.','Build a three-colour palette and test five text and background pairs.','All body text pairs score 4.5 to 1 or higher and the results are recorded.','What minimum contrast ratio should body text meet?','At least 4.5 to 1.'),
 M('Grid, spacing and alignment','Apply',5,['Set margins first, for example 12 mm on all sides for a print page.','Divide the page into a grid of 4 or 6 columns and snap items to it.','Use spacing in steps, for example 8, 16 and 32 pixels, and repeat them.','Align edges so that the eye finds clear lines.'],'A social post of 1080 by 1080 pixels uses a 60 pixel margin and a 4 column grid; logo, headline and button all sit on column edges.','Redo a messy layout on a grid and compare before and after.','All items align to the grid and spacing uses no more than three set values.','What does a grid give a designer?','Order, alignment and a faster way to decide where things go.'),
 M('Logo and brand kit','Create',6,['Sketch at least 20 small ideas on paper before using the computer.','Draw the logo as vectors so it can scale from a small stamp to a large board.','Make versions in full colour, one colour, black and white, and reversed.','Write a one-page guide with colours as codes, fonts, minimum size and clear space.'],'A logo works at 20 mm wide on a bill and 2 metres on a shop board because it is a vector; the guide sets clear space equal to the height of the first letter.','Create a logo and a one-page brand guide for a practice business.','Logo reads at 20 mm, has four versions, and the guide lists codes, fonts and clear space.','Why draw a logo as vectors?','Vectors scale to any size without losing sharpness.'),
 M('Print and screen files','Apply',5,['Set print files at 300 pixels per inch with 3 mm bleed and keep text 5 mm inside the trim.','Export screen files as compressed images or vectors sized to where they will appear.','Convert text to outlines or embed fonts when sending files to a printer.','Name files by project, size and version and keep an editable master.'],'A poster for A4 is 2480 by 3508 pixels at 300 ppi; a web banner of 1200 by 300 pixels is kept under 200 KB.','Prepare one print file and one screen file from the same design.','Print file has bleed and correct resolution; screen file is the right size and under the size limit set.','Why add bleed to a print file?','So trimming does not leave white edges.'),
 M('Capstone: brand kit and three layouts','Create',5,['Write a brief and build a logo, palette and type pairing for a practice client.','Design a poster, a social post and a one-page flyer from the same kit.','Test contrast, sizes and alignment and record the results in a checklist.','Export print and screen files and present the work with reasons for each choice.'],'A kit with one logo set, three colours, two typefaces and three layouts; every text pair scores above 4.5 to 1 and files are named and exported.','Present the kit to the client and revise once.','Designs match the brief, checks are recorded and files open and print correctly.','What proves a design works?','It meets the brief, passes the readability checks and the intended audience understands it.')],
 extra=[('Why keep an editable master file?','So changes can be made later without redrawing.'),('Why show the client your reasons?','Reasons turn taste arguments into decisions based on the brief.')],
 rubric=RUB('Brief and hierarchy','Brief approved and hierarchy tested with viewers','Readability and files','Contrast and sizes measured and files print correctly'),
 ladder=LAD('Notice type, colour and layout in everyday signs.','Explain hierarchy, contrast and grid in plain words.','Copy and improve simple layouts with guidance.','Design posters and posts from a brief alone.','Build brand kits and manage several clients.','Lead a design team and set quality checks.','Set design standards and train designers across a business.'),
 jobs=['Graphic designer','Social media designer','Print and packaging designer','Brand designer','Freelance visual designer']),

dict(slug='childcare', title='Childcare Products and Services', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Choose, test and offer safe products and services for babies and young children, and run a small childcare service with care and records.',
 outs=[('Define','the stages of child development from birth to six.'),('Apply','safety checks to toys, furniture and spaces.'),('Analyse','a product for age fit and risk.'),('Evaluate','a service against safeguarding rules.'),('Create','a daily plan and a safety file.')],
 task='Review a childcare product and run a small day plan for young children, with safety checks and records.', cond='About 8 weeks part-time. A play space or creche visit, product samples, a supervisor.',
 standard='Every check is written and dated, hazards are removed or reported within one day, staff to child ratios are met, and the daily plan fits the age group.', who='Parents, carers, early-years assistants, small business owners.', req='None.',
 mods=[
 M('Child development from birth to six','Define',4,['List key milestones: smiles at 2 months, sits at 6 months, walks at about 12 months, joins words at about 2 years.','Group needs into body, language, thinking, feelings and social skills.','Remember that children vary and a gap of a few months is common; note when to refer a concern.','Match play to stage: touch and sound for babies, building and pretend play for toddlers, rules and stories for older children.'],'A 9-month-old explores by mouthing, so any toy must be too big to swallow; a 4-year-old can follow a three-step game.','Make a one-page chart of stages with a play idea for each.','Chart covers birth to six years in five areas with one safe play idea per stage.','Why can two healthy children reach a milestone months apart?','Children develop at different speeds, so a range is normal.'),
 M('Safe toys and products','Analyse',5,['Check the age label and look for small parts: nothing that fits through a tube of 3.2 cm across for children under three.','Look for sharp edges, loose parts, long cords over 22 cm, button batteries and magnets.','Check materials for safe paint and no strong smell; read the care label.','Test by pulling, twisting and dropping, then record pass or fail.'],'A toy car loses a wheel after 10 pulls; the wheel fits the 3.2 cm tube, so it is failed for under threes.','Test five toys with a written checklist.','Each toy has a signed result and failed items are removed from use.','What is the small parts test?','Anything that fits through a 3.2 cm tube can choke a child under three.'),
 M('Safe sleep, feeding and hygiene','Apply',5,['Place babies to sleep on their back on a firm flat surface with no pillows, loose blankets or soft toys.','Wash hands for 20 seconds before feeding and after nappy changes or toilet use.','Sterilise bottles, store prepared milk as advised and discard what is left after a feed.','Record feeds and sleeps and note anything unusual.'],'A feed log for one baby shows 6 feeds in 24 hours at 90 ml each; a sudden drop to 40 ml is reported to the parent the same day.','Write a routine card for sleep, feeds and hand washing and follow it for a week.','Routine is followed daily and logged without gaps.','How long should hands be washed?','About 20 seconds with soap and water.'),
 M('Safe spaces and furniture','Evaluate',5,['Walk the space at child height and list hazards: sockets, sharp corners, hot items, stairs, water.','Check cots, high chairs and gates for stable build and slats no wider than 6 cm apart.','Keep cleaning liquids and medicines locked away and out of sight.','Plan exits and a fire route, and practise it.'],'A room walk finds 11 hazards; 9 are fixed that day with covers and corner guards, and 2 are reported for repair by the owner.','Audit a room and write a hazard list with actions and dates.','Every hazard has an owner and a due date, and fixed items are checked again.','Why inspect a room from child height?','Children reach and see different things than adults do.'),
 M('First aid and emergencies','Apply',5,['Learn choking response by age, for a baby back blows and chest thrusts, and call for help early.','Know how to cool a burn under running water for 20 minutes.','Keep a stocked first aid kit and check it monthly against a list.','Keep emergency numbers and parent contacts on the wall and write every incident down.'],'A kit checklist of 15 items shows 2 plasters missing and one expired item, which are replaced the same week.','Run a practice drill and complete an incident form.','Drill is completed in the time set and the form has date, time, action and parent informed.','How long should a burn be cooled?','About 20 minutes under running water.'),
 M('Safeguarding and respectful care','Evaluate',5,['Never leave a child alone with anyone who is not cleared; use two adults for bathroom help where possible.','Know signs of harm: sudden fear, unexplained marks, big changes in behaviour.','Report concerns to the supervisor at once and write facts without opinions.','Respect privacy: share information only with parents and those who need it.'],'A child has the same bruise pattern on two days; the carer writes date, place and the exact words of the child and tells the supervisor the same day.','Write a safeguarding policy page and role-play one report.','Policy names who to tell, report is factual and the child is kept safe.','What should a report of concern contain?','Facts only: date, what was seen or said and who was told.'),
 M('Daily plan and activities','Create',5,['Build a day with a steady rhythm of play, meals, rest and outdoor time.','Keep ratios, for example one adult to three children under two, and one adult to eight children aged three to six.','Plan one activity for language, one for movement and one for creativity, each ten to twenty minutes.','Prepare a rainy day option and a quiet option.'],'A morning for six toddlers with two adults: arrival 15 minutes, songs 15, free play 40, snack 20, outdoor 30, story 15, rest 45.','Write a day plan for a group and run one morning.','Plan fits the age, ratio is met and times add up to the session length.','Why keep a steady daily rhythm?','Children feel secure when they know what comes next.'),
 M('Capstone: safety file and day plan','Create',5,['Review one product and one space and record the findings in a safety file.','Write a day plan and run it with a supervisor.','Record attendance, incidents and feedback for one week.','Present risks found, fixes made and what you would change.'],'A file with 5 tested products, a room audit with 11 hazards closed, a one-week log and a day plan with ratio 1 to 3 for toddlers.','Present the file and answer questions.','File is complete, dated and signed; no hazard is left without action.','What shows a service is safe?','Written checks, fixed hazards, correct ratios and clear records.')],
 extra=[('Why keep written records of checks?','They prove checks happened and help spot repeat problems.'),('Why tell parents about small incidents?','Parents need full information and trust grows from honesty.')],
 rubric=RUB('Safety checks','Written, dated and acted on within one day','Daily plan','Fits age, ratio and time with a backup option'),
 ladder=LAD('Know basic child safety at home.','Explain the stages of development and safe sleep.','Help in a childcare setting under supervision.','Run a small group safely and keep records.','Lead a childcare service and train helpers.','Manage several sites and audit safeguarding.','Set childcare safety standards and train providers across a region.'),
 jobs=['Childcare assistant','Creche worker','Nanny','Childcare product reviewer','Day care owner']),

dict(slug='enrichment-for-children', title='Enrichment Activities for Children', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Design and lead after-school and holiday activities in art, science, sport, music and reading that build skills and confidence safely.',
 outs=[('Define','the skill each activity builds.'),('Apply','a session plan with safety and inclusion.'),('Analyse','what a child gained from a session.'),('Evaluate','an activity for age fit and cost.'),('Create','a four-week activity programme.')],
 task='Plan and lead a four-week enrichment programme for a group of children, with a record of the progress of each child.', cond='About 8 weeks part-time. A group of 8 to 15 children, a safe space, a supervisor.',
 standard='Each session has one skill aim, a written risk check, an adult to child ratio of at most 1 to 10, and a record showing at least 80 percent of children finished the task.', who='Teachers, youth leaders, parents, students.', req='None.',
 mods=[
 M('Skills that activities build','Define',4,['Name the skill: fine motor, balance, teamwork, creativity, reasoning, listening or confidence.','Write the aim as what a child will do, for example fold, build, sing or explain.','Match the skill to the age; a 6-year-old can cut along a line, a 12-year-old can design a test.','Choose activities that stretch a child a little without causing fear of failure.'],'Aim for ages 6 to 8: By the end of the session each child folds a paper boat with 6 steps and floats it for 10 seconds.','Write aims for five activities in plain verbs.','Each aim names one skill, one observable action and one measure.','Why write the aim as an action the child does?','So you can see and count whether it happened.'),
 M('Art and craft sessions','Apply',5,['Use low-cost and recycled material such as cardboard, cloth scraps and leaves.','Show one finished sample, then demonstrate in three steps.','Set out materials in trays so each child has the same, and allow free choice in the last ten minutes.','Use non-toxic paint and glue and keep scissors for children with blunt tips.'],'A 45-minute session: 5 minutes sample, 10 minutes demo, 25 minutes making a mask from cardboard, 5 minutes display.','Lead a craft session for six children and record outcomes.','At least 5 of 6 children finish a product and the space is cleared in 5 minutes.','Why show a sample first?','Children see what is expected and start faster.'),
 M('Science through play','Apply',5,['Pick a question children can test with safe items, such as which material floats.','Ask them to predict, test, record and explain, in that order.','Use no flames, mains power or strong chemicals with young children.','Use simple tables and drawings to record results.'],'Test 6 objects in a tub: 4 float and 2 sink; children record in a table and discover that heavy size alone does not decide.','Run an investigation with a prediction table.','Every child records a prediction and a result and explains one finding.','What order should a simple investigation follow?','Predict, test, record, explain.'),
 M('Sport and movement','Apply',5,['Start with a 5 to 10 minute warm-up and finish with a cool-down and water.','Mark clear boundaries and keep activity space free of stones and holes.','Use small-sided games, for example 4 against 4, so every child touches the ball often.','Stop play in heat; in hot weather, move activity to shade or morning hours.'],'A game of 3 against 3 gives each child about 20 touches in 10 minutes, against 4 touches in 11 against 11.','Plan and lead a 40-minute movement session.','Session has warm-up, game and cool-down, and each child is active for at least 30 minutes.','Why use small-sided games?','Each child gets more turns and more practice.'),
 M('Music, stories and reading','Apply',5,['Use rhythm, clapping and simple songs in the home language and one other language.','Read aloud for 10 minutes, pause to ask what might happen next.','Let children retell or act out the story in their own words.','Offer books at three levels so every child can pick one.'],'After a 10-minute story, 8 of 10 children retell the beginning, middle and end using the picture cards.','Lead a story-and-song session and note retelling.','At least 80 percent retell the story in order.','Why pause to ask what happens next?','It builds thinking and keeps children engaged.'),
 M('Inclusion, behaviour and safety','Evaluate',5,['Adapt each task so a child with a different need can take part, for example a seat for a child who cannot stand long.','Set three simple rules with children at the start and praise what you want to see.','Do a written risk check of the space, materials and ratio before every session.','Record accidents and inform parents the same day; keep medicine and allergy information on file.'],'A risk check before a game finds a wet floor; it is dried and the game starts 5 minutes late, which is better than a fall.','Write a risk checklist and an inclusion plan for one session.','Every child can join and every risk has a control.','What goes into a risk check?','Space, materials, ratio and each hazard with its control.'),
 M('Observe, record and give feedback','Analyse',5,['Use a simple sheet: name, aim, done or not, one note.','Count how many reached the aim and look for patterns by age or group.','Give praise for effort and a clear next step, not a score or a comparison.','Share progress with parents each fortnight in a short note.'],'After 4 sessions, 11 of 14 children finished the task; 3 needed more time, so the next session adds 10 minutes.','Keep a record for two sessions and write one parent note.','Record covers every child and the next session changes because of it.','Why give feedback on effort rather than comparison?','It builds confidence and keeps children willing to try.'),
 M('Capstone: four-week programme','Create',5,['Write a four-week plan with one aim and one skill per session.','Prepare a budget list of materials in units and quantity, and a risk check.','Lead the sessions with a supervisor and keep records.','Evaluate results and write what to keep, change or drop.'],'Four weekly sessions of art, science, sport and story for 12 children; completion rises from 70 percent in week 1 to 90 percent in week 4.','Present the programme and results.','Plan, risk checks and records are complete and results guide the changes.','What shows the programme worked?','Records of completion and skills, and happy children who keep coming.')],
 extra=[('Why test an activity before leading it?','You find missing steps and hazards and check the timing.'),('Why involve children in setting rules?','They understand and follow rules they helped make.')],
 rubric=RUB('Aims and planning','Each session has one measurable aim and timed steps','Safety and inclusion','Risk check and adaptation for every child, every session'),
 ladder=LAD('Know simple games and crafts for children.','Explain how play builds skills.','Assist an activity leader.','Plan and lead sessions alone.','Design programmes and train helpers.','Lead a team of activity leaders.','Set programme and safety standards across centres.'),
 jobs=['Activity leader','After-school club coordinator','Holiday camp organiser','Youth worker','Early-years enrichment tutor']),
]
